// Format-string errors that are PASS-2 diagnostics, so they need a file with no pass-1
// error in it.  The parser skips pass 2 the moment pass 1 reports an error
// (`Parser::parse`), and a name is resolved only when `!first_pass`; sharing a file with
// 35-format-errors.loft's pass-1 spec refusals is what kept these silently unasserted
// (loft#929).

// A bare unknown specifier has no width digits in front of it, so it could be a WIDTH
// EXPRESSION — a name.  It names nothing, so it is reported as the spec it was meant as,
// not as *"Unknown variable 'z'"* (loft#1805).  Pass 2 only: pass 1 has not yet met a
// variable declared further down, so it cannot tell.  (With digits, as in `{n:5z}`, the
// specifier grammar owns the `z` on both passes; that twin lives in 35-format-errors.loft.)
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `z` is not a format spec
fn test_bare_specifier_z() {
  n = 42;
  s = "{n:z}";
}

// A radix letter in the wrong case names the one meant (loft#1805).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `B` is not a format spec — the spec is lower-case `b`
fn test_bare_uppercase_b() {
  n = 42;
  s = "{n:B}";
}

// A name interpolated BEFORE its assignment is undefined at the point it is read —
// the format string does not defer the lookup to the end of the block.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Unknown variable 'cd'
fn test_format_use_before_assign() {
  s = "{cd}";
  cd = 5;
}
